data.content:
The lightest approach for .NET 8+ is the built-in **`TimeProvider`** class — no custom interfaces, no DI container needed.

### The pattern

Accept `TimeProvider` as an optional constructor parameter defaulting to the real clock:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
    {
        _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;
    }

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

### In tests

Add the NuGet package `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`, then:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-5)); // you can move time backward too
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 3, 2, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

### Why this is the lightest option

| Concern | Answer |
|---------|--------|
| Custom interface? | No — `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+) |
| DI container? | Not needed — optional param defaults to real clock |
| Existing callers break? | No — `new InvoiceCalculator()` still works |
| Test package | One small NuGet: `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` |

